            <ArticleTitle>Giant Caudate Lobe Hemangioma.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1957-1958</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s11605-017-3473-3</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Liver hemangiomas are the most common benign liver mass and typically asymptomatic and incidentally found. Giant liver hemangiomas are defined as having an overall diameter of &gt;5 cm and more likely to be symptomatic. We present a case of a dominant caudate lobe hemangioma presented with weight loss and abdominal pain.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
